WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.839
A secure Axum application is more than its
handlers.

2
00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:08.396
Crates, feature flags, middleware
dependencies, database clients,

3
00:00:08.476 --> 00:00:12.725
session libraries, serialization
libraries, TLS integrations,

4
00:00:12.805 --> 00:00:16.903
and proxy integrations become part of the
trusted application.

5
00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:20.473
Dependency review should consider what
code is added,

6
00:00:20.553 --> 00:00:24.423
which features are enabled, what parsing
behavior changes,

7
00:00:24.503 --> 00:00:27.462
and what runtime assumptions are
introduced.

8
00:00:27.542 --> 00:00:33.006
Configuration should be validated before
the service accepts traffic or at a clearly

9
00:00:33.086 --> 00:00:34.300
defined boundary.

10
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.705
Missing secrets, unsafe defaults,
unexpected environment modes,

11
00:00:38.785 --> 00:00:43.641
disabled protections, and incorrect
endpoint settings should fail early.

12
00:00:43.721 --> 00:00:48.957
Configuration that changes security
behavior deserves documentation, review,

13
00:00:49.037 --> 00:00:50.934
and operational visibility.

14
00:00:51.014 --> 00:00:56.326
Secrets should be delivered through
approved mechanisms and treated as sensitive

15
00:00:56.406 --> 00:00:57.999
everywhere they travel.

16
00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:02.556
They should not appear in source code,
responses, logs, crash output,

17
00:01:02.636 --> 00:01:05.368
build artifacts, images, or diagnostics.

18
00:01:05.448 --> 00:01:08.863
Redaction should be deliberate rather than
hopeful.

19
00:01:08.943 --> 00:01:11.598
When teams add structured logs or traces,

20
00:01:11.678 --> 00:01:16.155
they should decide which fields are safe,
which fields are sensitive,

21
00:01:16.235 --> 00:01:18.967
and which fields should never be recorded.

22
00:01:19.047 --> 00:01:22.613
Deployment assumptions affect application
security.

23
00:01:22.693 --> 00:01:26.715
TLS termination, reverse proxy behavior,
forwarded headers,

24
00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:31.196
trusted networks, exposed ports, health
checks, readiness checks,

25
00:01:31.276 --> 00:01:34.236
background workers, environment variables,

26
00:01:34.316 --> 00:01:37.123
and monitoring all shape the real
boundary.

27
00:01:37.203 --> 00:01:40.466
The code and deployment should tell the
same story.

28
00:01:40.546 --> 00:01:43.202
If the application trusts a proxy header,

29
00:01:43.282 --> 00:01:47.455
the infrastructure must enforce where that
header can come from.

30
00:01:47.535 --> 00:01:50.798
Monitoring and incident readiness close
the loop.

31
00:01:50.878 --> 00:01:55.659
Secure services emit useful, sanitized
signals about failures, denials,

32
00:01:55.739 --> 00:02:01.126
latency, dependency health, unexpected
request patterns, and resource pressure.

33
00:02:01.206 --> 00:02:03.103
The goal is not noisy logging.

34
00:02:03.183 --> 00:02:07.660
The goal is enough observability that
operators can detect problems,

35
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.900
investigate safely, and improve the
service after operational lessons appear.
